WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.625 --> 00:00:03.962
A mummy is defined broadly
as any well-preserved cadaver,

00:00:04.045 --> 00:00:05.588
and has a history that extends

00:00:05.672 --> 00:00:08.007
to almost every
continent on the globe.

00:00:08.091 --> 00:00:09.384
But when we're talking monsters,

00:00:09.467 --> 00:00:11.511
it's hard not to
picture anything

00:00:11.594 --> 00:00:14.347
that's not your gauzy,
wrapped, upright human.

00:00:14.431 --> 00:00:16.474
Why did the Egyptian mummy

00:00:16.558 --> 00:00:19.227
become the poster child
for mummification?

00:00:19.310 --> 00:00:22.063
And how did an ancient
common funerary rite

00:00:22.147 --> 00:00:23.773
turn into the shambling,
silent, linen-wrapped,

00:00:23.857 --> 00:00:27.402
undead corpse monster
of popular culture?

00:00:27.485 --> 00:00:29.112
[adventurous music]

00:00:29.195 --> 00:00:32.657
I'm Dr. Emily Zarka,
and this is "Monstrum."

00:00:32.741 --> 00:00:35.410
Mummification was a sacred,

00:00:35.493 --> 00:00:38.204
transformative practice
in ancient Egypt.

00:00:38.288 --> 00:00:41.040
Directly linked to the
gods Isis and Osiris,

00:00:41.124 --> 00:00:43.585
the ritual process made
one's body and soul

00:00:43.668 --> 00:00:46.171
fit for existence
in the afterlife.

00:00:46.254 --> 00:00:48.840
Part of the mummy's journey
into the stuff of horrors

00:00:48.923 --> 00:00:50.759
began with the early
commodification

00:00:50.842 --> 00:00:51.760
of Egyptian human remains,

00:00:51.843 --> 00:00:55.388
and a little
etymological confusion.

00:00:55.472 --> 00:00:57.307
In early modern Asia and Greece,

00:00:57.390 --> 00:00:59.851
bitumen was a common
medical remedy.

00:00:59.934 --> 00:01:01.394
The Persian word for Bitumen,

00:01:01.478 --> 00:01:03.188
became the Latin mumiya,

00:01:03.271 --> 00:01:05.732
and later mummie
in medieval Europe.

00:01:05.815 --> 00:01:08.443
And with the word mummy
came the mistaken belief

00:01:08.526 --> 00:01:10.653
that mummified human corpses

00:01:10.737 --> 00:01:13.782
had the same medical
properties as bitumen,

00:01:13.865 --> 00:01:16.409
thus creating a corpse
trade where human mummies

00:01:16.493 --> 00:01:18.953
were harvested for use
in pharmaceuticals.

00:01:19.037 --> 00:01:20.538
By the 16th century,

00:01:20.622 --> 00:01:22.832
the controversial trade
became increasingly popular

00:01:22.916 --> 00:01:26.044
as people used dead human
remains as medicine.

00:01:26.127 --> 00:01:27.170
One English merchant's account

00:01:27.253 --> 00:01:29.547
of his handling of
an Egyptian mummy

00:01:29.631 --> 00:01:31.216
illustrates the
kind of treatment

00:01:31.299 --> 00:01:33.051
the remains were subject to

00:01:33.134 --> 00:01:35.512
in this bizarre trade industry.

00:01:35.595 --> 00:01:37.430
John Sanderson writes that he

00:01:37.514 --> 00:01:39.682
"broke off all
parts of the bodies

00:01:39.766 --> 00:01:42.644
to see how the flesh
was turned to drugge,"

00:01:42.727 --> 00:01:46.022
bringing home more than
600 pounds of remains

00:01:46.105 --> 00:01:48.608
for trade in London
apothecaries.

00:01:48.691 --> 00:01:50.693
Some of these specimens
were wholly intact

00:01:50.777 --> 00:01:54.364
and pharmacies would unwrap the
bodies for scientific study.

00:01:54.447 --> 00:01:57.867
In 1763, John Hadley
unrolled a mummy in his home

00:01:57.951 --> 00:02:00.495
at the behest of the
British Royal Society.

00:02:00.578 --> 00:02:03.790
His documentation helped lead
to the first systematic study

00:02:03.873 --> 00:02:05.917
of Egyptian mummy necropsies

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:08.878
by German physician Johann
Friedrich Blumenbach

00:02:08.962 --> 00:02:10.922
in the late 1700s.

00:02:11.005 --> 00:02:13.466
For centuries, ancient
Egypt was a subject

00:02:13.550 --> 00:02:14.968
of intrigued to Europeans,

00:02:15.051 --> 00:02:19.138
and Napoleon's 1798 to
1801 invasion of Egypt

00:02:19.222 --> 00:02:21.683
brought back an influx
of pillaged artifacts

00:02:21.766 --> 00:02:23.518
and firsthand accounts,

00:02:23.601 --> 00:02:27.730
fueling intrigue and sparking
a field of study, Egyptology.

00:02:27.814 --> 00:02:29.399
This marked the first grips

00:02:29.482 --> 00:02:32.235
of Egyptomania in
the 19th century,

00:02:32.318 --> 00:02:35.321
a term used to summarize
the robust popular interest

00:02:35.405 --> 00:02:37.031
in ancient Egyptian culture.

00:02:37.115 --> 00:02:39.659
Ancient Egypt continued
as a fashionable pursuit

00:02:39.742 --> 00:02:41.828
in the first decade
of the 19th century,

00:02:41.911 --> 00:02:43.538
mostly in Britain.

00:02:43.621 --> 00:02:46.165
The British government
built its own Egyptian Hall,

00:02:46.249 --> 00:02:48.251
which housed artifacts
transferred to Britain

00:02:48.334 --> 00:02:50.503
as part of Napoleon's surrender.

00:02:50.587 --> 00:02:52.463
The public even
started to collect

00:02:52.547 --> 00:02:54.591
cheap replicas of
Egyptian furniture,

00:02:54.674 --> 00:02:57.343
china patterns inspired
by Egyptian designs,

00:02:57.427 --> 00:02:59.262
and other commercial items.

00:02:59.345 --> 00:03:01.764
The 1820s brought
significant contributions

00:03:01.848 --> 00:03:03.975
to the mummy monster as well.

00:03:04.058 --> 00:03:06.603
Former circus strongman
Giovanni Belzoni's

00:03:06.686 --> 00:03:08.521
recreation of an Egyptian tomb

00:03:08.605 --> 00:03:13.192
in Piccadilly's Egyptian
Hall In 1821 drew crowds,

00:03:13.276 --> 00:03:15.528
as did his inventive
marketing techniques,

00:03:15.612 --> 00:03:18.448
which included public
mummy autopsies.

00:03:18.531 --> 00:03:19.824
He was occasionally assisted

00:03:19.908 --> 00:03:21.951
by the surgeon and
antiquarian Thomas Pettigrew,

00:03:22.035 --> 00:03:25.121
who would become a
famous Egyptologist

00:03:25.204 --> 00:03:27.373
and celebrity mummy autopsist

00:03:27.457 --> 00:03:30.877
the following decade after
his 1834 publication,

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:32.754
"History of Egyptian Mummies,"

00:03:32.837 --> 00:03:36.799
considered the foundational
text in modern mummy studies.

00:03:36.883 --> 00:03:38.801
In 1822, John-Francois
Champollion

00:03:38.885 --> 00:03:41.971
translated hieroglyphics
for the first time

00:03:42.055 --> 00:03:43.640
using the Rosetta Stone.

00:03:43.723 --> 00:03:44.933
Desire to better understand

00:03:45.016 --> 00:03:47.226
all aspects of ancient
Egyptian culture,

00:03:47.310 --> 00:03:49.479
including its dead, grew.

00:03:49.562 --> 00:03:52.273
Attempts to identify the name
and social status of mummies

00:03:52.357 --> 00:03:54.817
became increasingly important.

00:03:54.901 --> 00:03:57.445
In 1825, Augustus
Bozzi Granville

00:03:57.528 --> 00:04:01.324
performed the first modern
medical autopsy of a mummy.

00:04:01.407 --> 00:04:03.743
The mummy continued to
turn up in hospitals,

00:04:03.826 --> 00:04:07.205
artist studios, dissection
theaters, universities,

00:04:07.288 --> 00:04:10.792
and on the drawing room
tables of the upper class.

00:04:10.875 --> 00:04:14.587
Unrolling or unwrapping mummies
was both an academic pursuit

00:04:14.671 --> 00:04:17.048
and a form of titillating
entertainment.

00:04:17.131 --> 00:04:18.967
Members of the public
who could not attend

00:04:19.050 --> 00:04:20.051
such a spectacle in person

00:04:20.134 --> 00:04:23.304
had their appetites whet
with detailed reports

00:04:23.388 --> 00:04:25.640
of the experience in newspapers.

00:04:25.723 --> 00:04:27.600
When you consider how
the removal of the corpse

00:04:27.684 --> 00:04:29.269
from its intended resting place

00:04:29.352 --> 00:04:31.729
turns it more
object than person,

00:04:31.813 --> 00:04:34.190
the treatment of the mummified
corpse as entertainment

00:04:34.274 --> 00:04:36.943
is significant in
understanding the mummy.

00:04:37.026 --> 00:04:39.612
They are made uneasy
representations of culture,

00:04:39.696 --> 00:04:41.531
history, and personhood,

00:04:41.614 --> 00:04:44.784
something fiction responds
to by making them monsters.

00:04:44.867 --> 00:04:46.869
Jane Loudon is the first author

00:04:46.953 --> 00:04:50.164
to write these tensions
into a fictional text.

00:04:50.248 --> 00:04:53.251
Her 1827 science fiction
novel, "The Mummy!

00:04:53.334 --> 00:04:55.795
Or A Tale of the
Twenty-Second Century,"

00:04:55.878 --> 00:04:59.132
is the story of a
reanimated Egyptian mummy.

00:04:59.215 --> 00:05:00.591
In the long, bizarre tale,

00:05:00.675 --> 00:05:02.802
the Mummy Cheops
serves as a terrifying

00:05:02.885 --> 00:05:05.013
yet instructive
undead character.

00:05:05.096 --> 00:05:07.098
The book was incredibly
commercially popular

00:05:07.181 --> 00:05:09.350
and reprinted multiple times.

00:05:09.434 --> 00:05:11.019
The influence of imperialism

00:05:11.102 --> 00:05:13.104
contributed to the
commodification

00:05:13.187 --> 00:05:15.273
and fetishization
of the mummy in Britain.

00:05:15.356 --> 00:05:17.567
Colonial territories in
other distant countries

00:05:17.650 --> 00:05:20.236
provided museums and
private collections

00:05:20.319 --> 00:05:24.032
with a steady stream of exotic
items, including bodies.

00:05:24.115 --> 00:05:26.117
By mid-century, the
unwrapping of a mummy

00:05:26.200 --> 00:05:29.829
had become both formulaic
and highly performative.

00:05:29.912 --> 00:05:31.164
The body would be
laid surrounded

00:05:31.247 --> 00:05:34.709
by other Egyptian artifacts
and funerary images.

00:05:34.792 --> 00:05:36.794
A lecture about the
practice of mummification

00:05:36.878 --> 00:05:38.379
and Egyptian history would serve

00:05:38.463 --> 00:05:40.506
as a prelude to the real show,

00:05:40.590 --> 00:05:44.635
the removal of the textiles
and other items on the body.

00:05:44.719 --> 00:05:46.429
Some of these items, an amulet,

00:05:46.512 --> 00:05:47.930
a fragment of bandage,
a piece of bone,

00:05:48.014 --> 00:05:50.725
might be passed around
for the audience

00:05:50.808 --> 00:05:53.770
to touch, smell, even taste.

00:05:53.853 --> 00:05:57.148
In some cases, particularly
at private unrolling parties,

00:05:57.231 --> 00:06:00.401
the guests may keep the
objects as souvenirs.

00:06:00.485 --> 00:06:03.863
We see this trend dramatized
in mummy fiction of the time.

00:06:03.946 --> 00:06:06.199
In French author
Theophile Gautier's story,

00:06:06.282 --> 00:06:07.533
"The Mummy's Foot,"

00:06:07.617 --> 00:06:09.160
a man buys a mummified foot

00:06:09.243 --> 00:06:12.914
and later a woman hopping on
one foot arrives at his door.

00:06:12.997 --> 00:06:14.874
She's an ancient
Egyptian princess

00:06:14.957 --> 00:06:16.918
searching for her
lost appendage,

00:06:17.001 --> 00:06:20.755
apparently unable to rest until
her full body is restored.

00:06:20.838 --> 00:06:23.049
Edgar Allen Poe's
1845 short story,

00:06:23.132 --> 00:06:24.717
"Some Words with a Mummy,"

00:06:24.801 --> 00:06:26.761
sets the reanimated
Egyptian corpse

00:06:26.844 --> 00:06:28.721
in an all too familiar place,

00:06:28.805 --> 00:06:30.848
the white man's
unwrapping party.

00:06:30.932 --> 00:06:32.308
The mummy at the
center of the story

00:06:32.391 --> 00:06:34.811
is brought back to life
with an electrical charge

00:06:34.894 --> 00:06:37.063
to the outright
terror of the guests.

00:06:37.146 --> 00:06:38.564
Louisa May Alcott is responsible

00:06:38.648 --> 00:06:40.900
for what many consider
to be the first

00:06:40.983 --> 00:06:42.485
mummy's curse narrative,

00:06:42.568 --> 00:06:45.863
"Lost in a Pyramid;
or, The Mummy's Curse."

00:06:45.947 --> 00:06:48.950
Penned in 1868, a mummified
Egyptian sorceress

00:06:49.033 --> 00:06:51.869
curses anyone who dares
to disturb her grave,

00:06:51.953 --> 00:06:53.162
which, of course,
two British men do.

00:06:53.246 --> 00:06:56.124
Famous mummy fiction at
the turn of the century,

00:06:56.207 --> 00:06:58.126
like Arthur Conan Doyle's
"The Ring of Thoth"

00:06:58.209 --> 00:06:59.877
and "Lot No. 249,"

00:06:59.961 --> 00:07:02.547
or Bram Stoker's "The
Jewel of Seven Stars"

00:07:02.630 --> 00:07:04.757
helped bolster the
already mysterious

00:07:04.841 --> 00:07:08.136
and mystical popular perception
of the Egyptian dead.

00:07:08.219 --> 00:07:09.762
The rise of the film industry

00:07:09.846 --> 00:07:13.391
continues to reinforce the
mummy as foreign antagonist.

00:07:13.474 --> 00:07:17.186
In the 1899 silent film
"Robbing Cleopatra's Tomb,"

00:07:17.270 --> 00:07:20.273
the mummy makes its first
appearance on screen.

00:07:20.356 --> 00:07:22.525
A man chops a female
mummy into pieces

00:07:22.608 --> 00:07:24.360
in order to resurrect her.

00:07:24.443 --> 00:07:26.070
In 1911's "The Mummy,"

00:07:26.154 --> 00:07:27.905
a female mummy comes to life

00:07:27.989 --> 00:07:30.575
after exposure
to an electrical current.

00:07:30.658 --> 00:07:31.868
The Egyptian princess

00:07:31.951 --> 00:07:33.828
is infatuated with
the male protagonist

00:07:33.911 --> 00:07:35.079
and aggressively pursues him,

00:07:35.163 --> 00:07:37.874
reinforcing the
whole Egyptian mummy

00:07:37.957 --> 00:07:39.584
as fetish commodity thing.

00:07:39.667 --> 00:07:42.962
The 1920s popularized the
idea of the mummy's curse.

00:07:43.045 --> 00:07:46.257
Although 19th century writers
had already used this trope,

00:07:46.340 --> 00:07:50.052
now the idea was supported
by rumors of a real curse.

00:07:50.136 --> 00:07:53.556
The rumor of King Tutankhamun's
curse is notorious,

00:07:53.639 --> 00:07:56.851
in part because some people
associated with the excavation

00:07:56.934 --> 00:07:58.853
did die in mysterious ways.

00:07:58.936 --> 00:08:00.271
When archeologist Howard Carter

00:08:00.354 --> 00:08:02.398
and his patron the
5th Earl of Carnarvon

00:08:02.481 --> 00:08:06.527
unburied the immense tomb of
King Tut in November 1922,

00:08:06.611 --> 00:08:09.197
it was an immediate
global sensation,

00:08:09.280 --> 00:08:11.616
sparking a new
period of Egyptomania

00:08:11.699 --> 00:08:15.036
that would grow into
a fever pitch in 1923

00:08:15.119 --> 00:08:18.956
when Lord Carnarvon fell ill
from an infected mosquito bite,

00:08:19.040 --> 00:08:21.375
after rumors circulated
that he had stolen

00:08:21.459 --> 00:08:24.462
a hoard of
valuable items from the dig.

00:08:24.545 --> 00:08:26.297
Even before the
lord's eventual death

00:08:26.380 --> 00:08:28.507
from complications
of the infection,

00:08:28.591 --> 00:08:31.969
stories circulated that he was
suffering a dire punishment

00:08:32.053 --> 00:08:34.388
for opening and robbing
the Egyptian king.

00:08:34.472 --> 00:08:36.557
While not the first
rumored mummy's curse,

00:08:36.641 --> 00:08:37.808
King Tut's was the curse

00:08:37.892 --> 00:08:39.977
with the most bodies
attributed to it,

00:08:40.061 --> 00:08:43.189
at least 12 if you include
some unfortunate pets.

00:08:43.272 --> 00:08:46.901
Real warnings do adorn the
walls of Egyptian tombs.

00:08:46.984 --> 00:08:48.402
Meant to deter thieves

00:08:48.486 --> 00:08:50.780
and keep the resting
place of the dead sacred,

00:08:50.863 --> 00:08:52.949
these hieroglyphics
never translate

00:08:53.032 --> 00:08:55.076
to vengeful undead corpses.

00:08:55.159 --> 00:08:57.328
In fact, in ancient
funerary tradition,

00:08:57.411 --> 00:09:00.039
the bones of the deceased
would be broken

00:09:00.122 --> 00:09:03.042
to prevent them from rising
and walking among the living.

00:09:03.125 --> 00:09:05.711
Life after death was one
thing for the Egyptians,

00:09:05.795 --> 00:09:07.922
disturbing the living
in this realm, however,

00:09:08.005 --> 00:09:09.632
was not encouraged.

00:09:09.715 --> 00:09:11.300
Besides the fact that
rumors of mummy curses

00:09:11.384 --> 00:09:14.011
make for profitable
tabloid fodder

00:09:14.095 --> 00:09:16.722
and easy inspiration
for gothic tales,

00:09:16.806 --> 00:09:19.016
it's important to note
that during this period,

00:09:19.100 --> 00:09:21.602
Egypt was actively
seeking independence

00:09:21.686 --> 00:09:23.646
from British colonial rule.

00:09:23.729 --> 00:09:25.815
Painting the physical
and bodily remains

00:09:25.898 --> 00:09:28.192
of the ancient Egyptians
in a negative way

00:09:28.276 --> 00:09:30.111
attempted to
discredit and demonize

00:09:30.194 --> 00:09:32.280
the living Egyptian population.

00:09:32.363 --> 00:09:34.031
While the film can be
read in several ways,

00:09:34.115 --> 00:09:36.075
it's hard to deny
the cultural impact

00:09:36.158 --> 00:09:39.370
of Universal Pictures'
1932 "The Mummy."

00:09:39.453 --> 00:09:41.914
Inspired in part by the
preceding decades' events

00:09:41.998 --> 00:09:45.001
as well as 19th century
gothic and horror tales,

00:09:45.084 --> 00:09:48.004
the film tells the story of
an undead Egyptian priest

00:09:48.087 --> 00:09:49.255
desperate to secure
the woman he believes

00:09:49.338 --> 00:09:52.216
to be the reincarnation
of his one-time lover.

00:09:52.300 --> 00:09:54.927
"The Mummy" is an
interesting commentary

00:09:55.011 --> 00:09:56.012
on the curse controversy

00:09:56.095 --> 00:09:58.180
around the opening
of King Tut's tomb,

00:09:58.264 --> 00:10:01.434
one that places science
as a dangerous pastime,

00:10:01.517 --> 00:10:04.228
at least when it comes
to disturbing corpses.

00:10:04.312 --> 00:10:06.105
Included with the
remains of Imhotep,

00:10:06.188 --> 00:10:08.107
the mummy at the
center of the film,

00:10:08.190 --> 00:10:10.651
is a box emblazoned
with a curse.

00:10:10.735 --> 00:10:12.194
Archeologist Whemple ignores

00:10:12.278 --> 00:10:15.197
occult scientist Professor
Muller's call for caution,

00:10:15.281 --> 00:10:17.283
declaring, "In the
interest of science,

00:10:17.366 --> 00:10:18.826
even if I believe in the curse,

00:10:18.909 --> 00:10:21.746
I'd go on with my
work for the museum."

00:10:21.829 --> 00:10:23.748
Although it's the
archeologist's assistant

00:10:23.831 --> 00:10:26.292
who inadvertently brings
the mummy back to life,

00:10:26.375 --> 00:10:28.377
the commentary on
the selfish pursuit

00:10:28.461 --> 00:10:31.130
of scientific advancement
and the folly of youth

00:10:31.213 --> 00:10:32.340
cannot be missed.

00:10:32.423 --> 00:10:34.925
And in the end it is magic,

00:10:35.009 --> 00:10:37.178
or rather, the movie's
warped interpretation

00:10:37.261 --> 00:10:39.221
of ancient Egyptian
spiritual practices

00:10:39.305 --> 00:10:41.098
that defeats the mummy.

00:10:41.182 --> 00:10:43.059
But what really made
the movie iconic,

00:10:43.142 --> 00:10:46.312
Boris Karloff's bandaged form.

00:10:46.395 --> 00:10:48.105
Even though the titular
mummy in the movie

00:10:48.189 --> 00:10:50.441
only spends a few
minutes on screen

00:10:50.524 --> 00:10:51.942
wrapped in linen bandages,

00:10:52.026 --> 00:10:53.569
appearing in the
rest of the film

00:10:53.652 --> 00:10:55.404
as a modern Egyptian historian,

00:10:55.488 --> 00:10:58.366
the compelling image animated
the mummy's public form

00:10:58.449 --> 00:11:02.620
in a way unseen since those
early 19th century necropsies.

00:11:02.703 --> 00:11:04.580
Yet it wasn't until the 1940s

00:11:04.663 --> 00:11:07.750
that the mummy became
a true horror monster.

00:11:07.833 --> 00:11:09.377
Where the Egyptian undead
had before

00:11:09.460 --> 00:11:12.546
been more moralistic,
romantic, or profound,

00:11:12.630 --> 00:11:13.756
even if they were placed

00:11:13.839 --> 00:11:16.092
in tension with
Western protagonists,

00:11:16.175 --> 00:11:19.804
this decade of horror films
made them something to fear.

00:11:19.887 --> 00:11:21.847
Their decay was
made more profound,

00:11:21.931 --> 00:11:23.724
often with wrappings
still in place,

00:11:23.808 --> 00:11:27.061
and they ambled about
seemingly void of all emotions

00:11:27.144 --> 00:11:29.105
other than anger and revenge.

00:11:29.188 --> 00:11:30.439
We see this trend take hold

00:11:30.523 --> 00:11:33.192
in Universal's second
foray into mummy horror,

00:11:33.275 --> 00:11:34.902
"The Mummy's Hand."

00:11:34.985 --> 00:11:36.028
Unable to secure Karloff

00:11:36.112 --> 00:11:39.156
for an official sequel
to their 1932 film,

00:11:39.240 --> 00:11:43.369
Universal gets creative with
the story...and the costuming.

00:11:43.452 --> 00:11:46.205
With an aim to capitalize
on audience recognition,

00:11:46.288 --> 00:11:48.416
they put the new actor
in a rubber mask,

00:11:48.499 --> 00:11:49.959
made the mummy mute,

00:11:50.042 --> 00:11:52.837
and kept him wrapped in
bandages for most of the movie.

00:11:52.920 --> 00:11:55.005
The odd shambling walk
that the Egyptian mummy

00:11:55.089 --> 00:11:56.424
would become known for?

00:11:56.507 --> 00:11:59.844
The replacement actor
Tom Tyler had arthritis.

00:11:59.927 --> 00:12:01.512
They gave the mummy
a new backstory

00:12:01.595 --> 00:12:04.223
to partially explain
these physical changes,

00:12:04.306 --> 00:12:05.558
getting rid
of the articulate mummy

00:12:05.641 --> 00:12:07.893
and replacing him
with a silent thief,

00:12:07.977 --> 00:12:10.354
cursed to be
an uncontrollable monster.

00:12:10.438 --> 00:12:13.315
The movie's sequel,
"The Mummy's Tomb" in 1942,

00:12:13.399 --> 00:12:16.694
reinforced the silent
shambling version of the mummy,

00:12:16.777 --> 00:12:19.989
and Universal will double down
on the image in the 1950s,

00:12:20.072 --> 00:12:22.950
but for the first time,
throw in some humor as well

00:12:23.033 --> 00:12:25.244
with "Abbott and
Costello Meet the Mummy."

00:12:25.327 --> 00:12:26.829
Just like the
advent of Egyptology

00:12:26.912 --> 00:12:29.165
and the medical autopsy
provided inspiration

00:12:29.248 --> 00:12:31.417
for tales some
hundred years before,

00:12:31.500 --> 00:12:33.961
scientific advancements
in the 1950s

00:12:34.044 --> 00:12:36.672
also found their way
into mummy myths.

00:12:36.755 --> 00:12:39.133
The greater prevalence
of x-rays in archeology

00:12:39.216 --> 00:12:41.135
and the use of chemical testing

00:12:41.218 --> 00:12:43.095
meant that the Egyptian
mummy could be examined

00:12:43.179 --> 00:12:45.306
in a way previously unheard of.

00:12:45.389 --> 00:12:47.641
Gone are the spells
or galvanic batteries

00:12:47.725 --> 00:12:49.518
that bring the mummy to life.

00:12:49.602 --> 00:12:51.729
Science's ability to
solve the mysteries

00:12:51.812 --> 00:12:53.439
of life and death
become paramount,

00:12:53.522 --> 00:12:56.108
not that such
scientific advances

00:12:56.192 --> 00:12:58.360
weren't again met
with trepidation.

00:12:58.444 --> 00:12:59.820
"Curse of the Faceless Man"

00:12:59.904 --> 00:13:02.198
claims radiation
from x-ray exposure

00:13:02.281 --> 00:13:04.116
is what revives the mummy.

00:13:04.200 --> 00:13:05.743
Another trend that arises,

00:13:05.826 --> 00:13:09.246
conflating the mummy with
other undead monsters,

00:13:09.330 --> 00:13:10.498
In the "Pharaoh's Curse,"

00:13:10.581 --> 00:13:12.958
the disturbance of the
remains of an Egyptian priest

00:13:13.042 --> 00:13:14.960
leads to a character
being possessed

00:13:15.044 --> 00:13:16.837
by the soul of the mummy.

00:13:16.921 --> 00:13:19.548
The character, aided by
a shapeshifting cat woman,

00:13:19.632 --> 00:13:23.260
begins rapidly aging and
starts sucking people's blood

00:13:23.344 --> 00:13:25.513
before becoming
the mummy himself.

00:13:25.596 --> 00:13:28.432
Since then, the mummy has
been a familiar horror figure

00:13:28.516 --> 00:13:31.185
in the pages of fiction,
children's cartoons,

00:13:31.268 --> 00:13:35.314
movies across genres, and on
the front of cereal boxes.

00:13:35.397 --> 00:13:38.025
They've been made frequent
characters in comic books,

00:13:38.108 --> 00:13:40.319
ravenous zombie-esque
flesh eaters,

00:13:40.402 --> 00:13:42.988
and Brendan Fraser's
sinewy nemesis.

00:13:43.072 --> 00:13:45.199
The Egyptian mummies
that stalk the pages

00:13:45.282 --> 00:13:48.035
and screens of horror,
science fiction, and fantasy

00:13:48.118 --> 00:13:51.163
are in a way justified
for their behavior.

00:13:51.247 --> 00:13:54.625
Rarely does a mummy animate
without outside provocation,

00:13:54.708 --> 00:13:57.086
and many of those bodies
are exposed to modernity

00:13:57.169 --> 00:13:59.838
by the mistakes or
greed of the living.

00:13:59.922 --> 00:14:02.216
They threaten to
literally consume or kill

00:14:02.299 --> 00:14:04.301
those who see them as objects

00:14:04.385 --> 00:14:05.886
ready for commercial consumption

00:14:05.970 --> 00:14:08.097
or scientific investigation.

00:14:08.180 --> 00:14:10.599
Part of what makes the
Egyptian mummy horrifying

00:14:10.683 --> 00:14:13.727
or monstrous outside
of its intended context

00:14:13.811 --> 00:14:15.646
is a lack of understanding

00:14:15.729 --> 00:14:17.356
associated with a death practice

00:14:17.439 --> 00:14:19.567
few people are familiar with,

00:14:19.650 --> 00:14:22.820
and mistreatment of those
bodies by outsiders.

00:14:22.903 --> 00:14:24.238
Before you go,
we want to invite you

00:14:24.321 --> 00:14:26.907
to participate in
PBS Digital Studios'

00:14:26.991 --> 00:14:28.534
annual audience survey.

00:14:28.617 --> 00:14:30.494
It's your chance
to tell PBS what you like

00:14:30.578 --> 00:14:32.621
and even vote
on new show ideas.

00:14:32.705 --> 00:14:34.957
If you'd like to continue
shaping the future of PBS

00:14:35.040 --> 00:14:36.709
There's a link in the
description below.

00:14:36.792 --> 00:14:39.169
We can't wait
to hear from you.

00:14:39.253 --> 00:14:40.254
Dramaticized.

00:14:40.337 --> 00:14:41.130
(speaker)
Dramatized.

00:14:41.213 --> 00:14:43.007
Dramatized, okay!

00:14:43.090 --> 00:14:44.758
Oh, is it Thoth?

00:14:44.842 --> 00:14:47.219
I did it right! [groaning]

00:14:47.303 --> 00:14:49.179
Carna, Carnarvon.

